{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS’ Customer Carbon Footprint Tool now includes Scope 3 emissions data - AWS","context":"AWS has updated its Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) to include Scope 3 emissions data and Scope 1 natural gas and refrigerants. This expansion allows AWS customers to have a more comprehensive view of their cloud carbon footprint, covering all three industry-standard emission scopes as defined by the Greenhouse Gas Protocol. The update provides visibility into the lifecycle carbon impact of AWS usage, including emissions from manufacturing servers, powering AWS facilities, and transporting equipment to data centers. Historical data is available from January 2022, enabling organizations to track their progress and make informed decisions about their cloud strategy to meet sustainability goals. The updated CCFT data is accessible through the dashboard and AWS Billing and Cost Management Data Exports, allowing for easy integration into operational workflows, sustainability planning, and reporting processes. For more information, visit the AWS Billing and Cost Management console or read the updated methodology documentation and release notes","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/aws-customer-carbon-footprint-tool-scope-3-emissions-data/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon S3 removes 30-day minimum for transitions to S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 has eliminated the 30-day minimum requirement for transitioning objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) and S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA). Objects can now be moved to these storage classes as soon as they are created. S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA offer up to 40% lower storage costs than S3 Standard while still providing quick access when needed. These storage classes are suitable for backups, log analytics, and compliance workloads where data becomes cold within hours or days. To initiate the transition, create new S3 Lifecycle rules. These rules can be established using the S3 console, AWS CLI, or SDKs. This update is accessible in all AWS Regions where S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA are offered. For pricing information, consult the Amazon S3 pricing page. For more details, refer to the overview page and documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/s3-removes-30-day-transitions-standard-ia-one-zone-ia/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"querying-aws-s3","skill_description":"Queries S3 object metadata, tracks bucket activity, audits object changes, searches annotations, and analyzes storage metrics using S3 Metadata system tables (journal, inventory, annotation) and S3 Storage Lens tables via Athena SQL. Applies when counting objects, finding recent uploads or deletions, identifying who wrote to a prefix, breaking down storage classes, finding objects by tag, searching annotation content, analyzing storage lens metrics, or enabling S3 Metadata tracking. Prefers system tables over raw S3 APIs (list-objects-v2, head-object) at scale. Trigger phrases: bucket activity, object count, who uploaded, track deletions, storage class breakdown, find by tag, search annotations, storage lens metrics, audit bucket changes.\n\nServices: s3, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-s3"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon S3 Tables now offer the Intelligent-Tiering storage class - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 Tables now supports the Intelligent-Tiering storage class, which optimizes storage costs based on access patterns without affecting performance or requiring additional operational overhead. Intelligent-Tiering automatically transitions data in tables between three low-latency access tiers (Frequent Access, Infrequent Access, and Archive Instant Access) as access patterns change. This results in potential cost savings of up to 80%. S3 Tables also perform automated maintenance operations, including compaction, snapshot expiration, and unreferenced file removal, to keep tables optimized while reducing storage costs. Data in tables not accessed for 30 consecutive days transitions to the Infrequent Access tier, and data not accessed for 90 days moves to the Archive Instant Access tier, which are 40% and 68% lower in cost, respectively, than the Frequent Access tier. Users can choose Intelligent-Tiering as the storage class when creating a table or set it as the default for all new tables in a table bucket. The Intelligent-Tiering storage class is available in all AWS Regions where S3 Tables are available. For pricing details, visit the Amazon S3 pricing page. For more information about S3 Tables, consult the product page, documentation, and the AWS News Blog","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/s3-tables-intelligent-tiering-storage-class/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-storage","skill_description":"Selects, investigates, and compares AWS object, file, and block storage services, and answers cost, performance, configuration, security, and troubleshooting questions about storage services. Applies when a user asks where to store or archive data based on their usage patterns; which storage service to choose or how two compare; how to migrate data from on-premises or between AWS services; how to protect, replicate, or recover data; how to optimize storage costs; where to deploy shared NFS, SMB, or POSIX file systems; where to store vector embeddings or tabular data; what storage backs enterprise file shares, self-managed databases on EC2, VMware, or stateful containers; or asks what an AWS storage service can do or how it works. Relevant for storage needs for workloads such as AI/ML, analytics, EDA, HPC, media, genomics, or financial trading. Not applicable for SQL query engines (Athena, Spark, Redshift, EMR), ETL (Glue), streaming (Kafka, MSK, Kinesis), or managed database services (RDS, Aurora, DynamoDB).\n\nServices: s3, efs, ebs, fsx\nTasks: decide, configure, create, optimize\nPersona: developer, architect, devops, it-administrator, storage-administrator, systems-engineer\nWorkload: storage","skill_name":"aws-storage"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Check AWS docs on Customer Carbon Footprint Tool reporting lag and methodology"}

